  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nCold advection pattern and upslope clouds and showers will\ncontinue tonight into early Tue. Eventually the shower activity\nwill dissipate in response to a shortwave trof approaching from\nnorth of the Great Lakes. At this time it looks like precip\nalong the warm front will remain north of the forecast area by\nand large. So I anticipate showers mainly along the cold front\nand then in its wake in the upslope zones again.\n\nA deep trof axis will move across the Great Lakes on Thursday \nas a sfc low develops near the OH Valley and then swings \noffshore of the Mid-Atlantic Thursday night into Friday morning.\nThis low is then progged to move northeastward towards Cape Cod\nlater Friday into Friday night before exiting towards the \nCanadian Maritimes on Saturday. A fairly large spread in \nguidance continues this evening in terms of the eventual track \nof this low and the severity of any possible impacts across the \nlocal region.\n\nLatest cluster analysis continues to favor an offshore track, \nwith most members of the EPS and AI guidance keeping the low \nwell to the SE of Cape Cod. That being said, there are some \noutliers with a more westward track, which would bring more \ndirect impacts to the area. The latest ECMWF EFI for snow does \nshow a shift of tales for portions of ME and NH but with no \nshading for EFI. This indicates that a few outlier ensemble \nmembers show snow for the region but most guidance does not. \nLatest ensemble and AI based probabilities for QPF of >=0.50\" \nare generally 30% or less, highest south of the mtns. Therefore,\nthe main takeaway is that while this systems bears watching \nover the next few days, the likelihood for a direct impact with \nmore significant amounts of rain, snow, and wind are lessening. \nGiven these uncertainties, the latest NBM seemed like a \nreasonable middle ground approach. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIn the wake of the coastal storm deep northerly flow will drag\ndown some colder air for the weekend into early next week.\nInitially some of the north northeast wind gusts near the coast\nespecially may be 30 to 35 mph depending on the storm track.\nOtherwise temps will drop back down to near to below normal.\nSome adjustments may need to be made to overnight temps, as NBM\nguidance is opting for radiational cooling bias correction in\na pattern more conducive for partially mixed boundary layer.",
